  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 422 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northern Saskatchewan,\nwith cold front stretching off to the south and west. High\nremains over the Upper Midwest into the Central Plains. Upper\nlevel analysis places weak trough over the Upper Midwest, with\nridging over the Northern Rockies into the western edge of the\nNorthern Plains ahead of a progressing trough over British\nColumbia. Over our area, quiet weather continues.\n\nThe aforementioned upper trough digging into British Columbia \npushes southeastward, with transient upper ridge passing over \ninto our area. Temperatures will be notably warmer, with highs \nin the 80s over most locations, and dry conditions continuing.\nFire weather concerns with this are addressed in a \"Fire\nWeather\" section below.\n\nPreviously mentioned cold front pushes through late tonight \ninto Friday morning, resulting in increasing northwesterly winds\nin its wake. With the easterly pushing upper trough, a few \nshowers may develop, primarily over northern locations. \n\nNotably cooler temperatures are then expected this weekend into\nearly next week. Quiet weather is expected through the day on\nSaturday, before a Pacific low quickly moves onshore and pushes\ntowards the Rockies, while a surface low develops to the lee of\nthe Rockies and gradually pushes easterly. This will bring\nincreasing precipitation chances to our area from Saturday night\nthrough the first part of the upcoming week. Guidance returns\ndrier weather to the area by Tuesday with the system lifting off\nto the northeast, though additional low precipitation chances\nreturn for mid-week.",
